    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>, who supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> but abhorred the violence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, is widely regarded as the philosophical founder of modern conservatism; Chateaubriand first used the term in politics in 1818.</li><li>Andrew Heywood names its five central beliefs as tradition, human imperfection, organic society, authority and hierarchy, and property, while Samuel P. Huntington called it a situational ideology that defends the established institutions of its time.</li><li>It has taken very different forms, from Disraeli’s One Nation Toryism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Bismarck</a>’s welfare state to the authoritarian regimes of Salazar and Franco and the Christian democratic parties that dominated post-war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>’s Conservative Party was formed in 1904 to stop the advent of universal suffrage, which its founders feared would bring socialism; yet it was a conservative admiral, Arvid Lindman, who first extended democracy in Sweden by enacting male suffrage.</p></aside>
    </section>

